A||||2||Fformula88||Re-attaching the firewall heat shield||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||01-24-2001||05:21 PM||||In the accident I had with our87 coupe last week, the heat shield on the engine compartment firewall broke loose.  All but one of the brakets that hold it on broke apart from excessive rust.  So there isn't anything left to attach it to.  My question... will it cause any problems to attach it directly to the exaust?  I have it on there right now, using exhaust clamps up top to get it away from the pipe a little and some at the bottom on either side of the cat converter to keep it from rattling.  It shields the whole system as it should so I forsee no problems.  It looks nearly impossible to reattach it directly to the firewall.  Does this sound like it will work?  Thanx.<P>------------------<BR>John<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">     <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||01-24-2001||05:37 PM||||almost everyone i know took them all off anyway, along with all the fibreglass like insulation, and if i remember rite, they attach onto the studs that hold the manifolds to the heads. You will always break off at least one of them trying to take em off, then your really screwed.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-24-2001||07:45 PM||||make some new brackets for it and epoxy them to the firewall. It's not hard. They don't have to be perfect. You do need to sand a spot clean for the epoxy to stick good. smear a film of epoxy over the whole spot so it doesn't rust then put some on the new bracket and hold it inplace 'til the glue start to set.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-24-2001).]||||reg||1
